## Quick Reference

**Before committing, always run:**

```bash
cargo fmt --all                                           # Format code
cargo clippy --all --all-features --tests -- -D warnings  # Lint (must pass)
cargo test                                                # Run tests
```

All three commands must pass without errors before code is considered ready.

## Project Layout
{% case layout %}{% when "bin" %}
```
{{project-name}}/
├── Cargo.toml   # Package manifest: metadata, dependencies, lints, profiles
└── src/
    └── main.rs  # Binary entry point
```

A single binary crate. Add modules as `src/<module>.rs` or `src/<module>/mod.rs`.
{% when "lib" %}
```
{{project-name}}/
├── Cargo.toml  # Package manifest: metadata, dependencies, lints, profiles
└── src/
    └── lib.rs  # Crate root and public API surface
```

A single library crate. Everything reachable from `lib.rs` is public API — see
[Documentation](#documentation).
{% when "workspace" %}
```
{{project-name}}/
├── Cargo.toml              # Workspace root: shared metadata, lints, profiles
└── crates/
    └── {{project-name}}/
        ├── Cargo.toml      # Member manifest
        └── src/
            └── main.rs     # Binary entry point
```

A Cargo workspace. Members live under `crates/` and are picked up by the
`crates/*` glob, so a new crate needs no edit to the root manifest.
{% when "members" %}
```
{{project-name}}/
├── Cargo.toml          # Workspace root: shared metadata, lints, profiles
└── {{project-name}}/
    ├── Cargo.toml      # Member manifest
    └── src/
        └── main.rs     # Binary entry point
```

A Cargo workspace whose members sit at the repository root. A new crate MUST be
added to `workspace.members` in the root `Cargo.toml`; there is no glob.
{% endcase %}{% if is_workspace %}
### Workspace Rules

- Shared metadata lives in `[workspace.package]`. Members MUST inherit it with
  `version.workspace = true`, `edition.workspace = true`, and so on. Only `name`
  and `description` are set per crate.
- Lints live in `[workspace.lints]`. Every member MUST opt in with:

  ```toml
  [lints]
  workspace = true
  ```

- Dependencies shared by more than one member SHOULD be declared in
  `[workspace.dependencies]` and referenced with `dep.workspace = true`.
{% endif %}
## Build & Run

```bash
cargo build                 # Build (debug)
cargo build --release       # Build (release, optimized)
{% if layout == "lib" %}cargo check                 # Check without building
cargo doc --open            # Build and open the API docs
{% elsif is_workspace %}cargo check --workspace     # Check without building

# Run the binary
cargo run -p {{project-name}}

# Run with less verbose logging
RUST_LOG=info cargo run -p {{project-name}}
{% else %}cargo run                   # Run the binary
RUST_LOG=info cargo run     # Run with less verbose logging
cargo check                 # Check without building
{% endif %}```

## Testing

```bash
cargo test                    # Run all tests{% if is_workspace %}, every member{% endif %}
cargo test test_name          # Run a single test by name
cargo test module_name::      # Run tests in a specific module
cargo test -- --nocapture     # Run tests with output
cargo test --lib              # Run only unit tests (no integration tests){% if layout == "lib" %}
cargo test --doc              # Run only doc-comment examples{% endif %}{% if is_workspace %}

# Run tests for a single crate
cargo test -p {{project-name}}{% endif %}
```

## Code Quality

### Formatting

```bash
cargo fmt --all               # Format all code
cargo fmt --all -- --check    # Check formatting without changing files
```

- Max line width: 100 characters
- Use 4 spaces for indentation
- Rust 2024 edition, format style edition 2024

### Linting

```bash
cargo clippy --all --all-features --tests -- -D warnings
```

The project has strict clippy lints enabled in {% if is_workspace %}`[workspace.lints]` in the
root `Cargo.toml`{% else %}`Cargo.toml`{% endif %}.

## Code Style Guidelines

### General Rules

- No `.unwrap()` in production code (allowed in tests) - use `?` or `.ok_or_else()`
- Use `parking_lot::{Mutex, RwLock}` instead of `std::sync` equivalents
- Use `Self` instead of repeating the type name
- Inline format args: `format!("{x}")` not `format!("{}", x)`

### Imports

Default behavior of `rustfmt`. Do not change the granularity of any imports and preserve the original structure written by the developer.

### Comments

Short sentences. RFC 2119 keywords for obligations. Commit = imperative subject; body only for a fact the diff cannot show. Comments only where code needs clarification — never narration.

### Struct and Enum Members

When members of a `struct`, `enum`, or similar item carry doc comments, separate each
member with a blank line. The comment and the member it describes read as one unit, and
without the blank line adjacent units run together.

Undocumented members need no blank line between them.

### Error Handling

- Use `thiserror` for library errors, `anyhow` for application errors
- Prefer `Result<T, E>` over panics
- Use `expect()` only when failure is truly impossible or during initialization
- Never use `unwrap()` in library code

### Logging

Use the `log` crate macros:

```rust
log::info!("...");
log::debug!("...");
log::warn!("...");
log::error!("...");
```

### Documentation

- Document all public APIs with `///` doc comments
- Include examples in doc comments for complex functions
- Use `//!` for module-level documentation
- Keep comments concise and up-to-date
{%- if layout == "lib" %}
- Every item exported from `lib.rs` MUST carry a `///` doc comment
- Doc examples are compiled and run by `cargo test`; keep them working
{%- endif %}
